Rewritten: the plugin's model-free summary -- the `duty' collectors in
dashboard/board.py and the text they render in dashboard/summary_text.py, the
way /duty in chat and `hermes duty` in the terminal both answered it -- as one
Nodecode cell. The same five things a person wants on coming back are the
board's five sections, in the same order: Waiting on you (a conversation whose
newest answer asked them something), Running (a turn in flight, with its
running time and the 10-minute quiet flag), Started, no answer (a turn that
began and left no reply -- the plugin's `you wrote, but no reply arrived'),
Cron trouble (a job whose last run failed, one paused, and a result nobody was
sent), and Standing goals. The one-line count the plugin printed in a busy
chat is `/duty counts', the two entry points are the same two -- the `duty'
tool for the model and /duty for the operator -- and, as there, reading
writes nothing and every source may be absent. The reading is this harness's
own: the session directory (title, source, newest turn, and the question the
harness recorded on the answer), the cron cell's registry row, and the goal
facts, through their public readers.

Left out, and why: the dashboard tab, because it is Hermes's web UI -- here
the board is a tool and a command, and `/duty' answers in the shell the same
way it answered in a Hermes DM. The 17 i18n catalogs and the right-to-left
layout went with the tab; so did the per-platform `owner' setting and the
one-to-one-DM rules, because this cell reads one home and every session in it
is the person's own, and the Discord jump links and the Seen
dismissals, which belonged to the cards of a page that no longer exists. The
plugin's question detection (its 40-line regular expression over the last
reply's tail, in several languages) is not reimplemented: the harness here
records what an answer waits on as a fact on the session, so the cell reads
that instead of guessing from prose. Its `background results' row also
survives only in the form this harness has -- a cron answer still owed --
because Hermes's async-delegation table has no counterpart here.
